work.prompt:
Question: ${question}
Atomic task: ${item}
Read only. Open sources rather than trusting snippets, prior context or other agents. Return individual one-fact claims with short verbatim excerpt, reference (local path/line or opened URL), source identity including the publication date the source shows, or 'undated' (never supply a date yourself), and sourceKind: primary when the source originated the fact or is its authoritative record (official docs or spec, source code, the author's own announcement, repository or paper, a standards body or registry); secondary for reporting or commentary about it (news, blogs, social posts, aggregators, wikis, third-party vendor pages). Before reporting 'undated', check the page itself and its metadata (JSON-LD datePublished, article:published_time or similar meta tags, an archive capture) for the publication date. Also return claims that limit, qualify or contradict the task's premise (warnings, counterexamples, when not to use something), not only supporting ones. Do not invent citations. If inaccessible or contested, report a gap; distinguish absence of evidence from disproof. Return only structured JSON {claims:[{claim,excerpt,reference,source,sourceKind}],gaps:[string]}.

evaluator.prompt:
Write follow-up research tasks for approved plan parts whose doneWhen bar is not yet met. Do not search, open sources or answer the question; write tasks only. Original question: ${question}
Approved plan parts with doneWhen bars: ${plan}
Evidence: ${feedback}
(items are worker results with ids r<iteration>-<index>, each with its task and its claims or error; gaps are the open gaps from the previous round.)
Write a task only for a gap that blocks an approved part's doneWhen; list every other gap without a task. Name gaps <partId>-<slug>. A topic outside every approved part is a gap named outside-<slug> with no task; the requester can start a follow-up run for it. Never write a task for a derived part. Each task uses an access route not yet tried in items: another primary source, the site's public API or mirror, an archive copy, an omitted upstream README or API/CLI source, or the earlier source an opened source cites. To check a disputed, consequential or weak claim, write a task that reopens its reference and quotes the passage. Never repeat a task already in items. List tasks most important first: the runtime runs only as many as the item budget allows. Return tasks: [] when no blocking gap has an untried accessible route, still listing every open gap. Return only {gaps:[{id,description}],tasks:[{gapId,item:{source,question,purpose,partIds}}]}.

synthesize.prompt:
Write a cited Markdown research report from the supplied evidence only.
Question: ${question}
Context (purpose, audience, constraints; unverified leads): ${context}
Plan parts with doneWhen bars: ${plan}
The Caller approved this plan: ${approved}
Seed evidence (round 0 leads): ${seed}
Research results: ${items}
(ids r<iteration>-<index>; each holds its task and its claims with claim, excerpt, reference, source and sourceKind, or an error.)
Open gaps: ${gaps}
Research stop reason: ${reason}
Evidence contract: the supplied evidence is the only approved source. Do not open, search, read or verify anything else, and write no files; return the result only through the structured output. Write for the requester in plain, specific prose. Structure: a short conclusion first; one section per plan part that answers it and states whether its doneWhen was met; limitations and open gaps, including outside-<slug> gaps the requester may research next; a Sources list. Cite each factual sentence inline as a Markdown link whose text is the result id and whose target is the claim's reference exactly as supplied, for example [r1-2](https://example.com/page); cite seed claims as [r0](reference). When a reference holds more than a URL (a note such as 'undated'), link only the URL and put the rest after the link. Never cite a reference no claim supplies. Take dates only from a claim's source or excerpt; never state access dates. Mark findings that rest only on secondary claims, on one source, or on disputed claims. If the Caller did not approve the plan, say so and treat affected parts cautiously. Report coverage once per plan part: met when its doneWhen is met, partial when useful evidence remains short of the bar, missing when nothing usable was found. Outcome follows coverage: succeeded when every part is met (omit reason); partial when useful findings remain but a part is partial or missing (reason names those parts); failed when no reliable findings remain. Return structured JSON only.
